# Keeps the Docker build context small.
#
# Without this file the context is 301 MB, 296 MB of which is node_modules —
# and the daemon has to hash and transfer all of it before the first
# instruction runs. It is pure waste: the Dockerfile installs dependencies
# inside the image from package.json and never copies node_modules in.
#
# It is also a correctness matter, not only speed. node_modules built on a
# developer's macOS arm64 machine contains native binaries (bcrypt) compiled
# for that host. Any COPY that pulled them into a linux/arm64 image would
# produce a container that fails at require() time, and the cause would not be
# obvious from the error.
#
# Anything the Dockerfile needs must NOT be listed here: package.json,
# tsconfig.json, src/, migrations/, database.json, winston-papertrail.d.ts.

# Dependencies — installed inside the image, never copied in
node_modules
**/node_modules

# Compiled output — the build stage runs tsc itself. This is tracked in git,
# which is the team's convention, but shipping a host-built dist into the image
# would mean deploying whatever happened to be on someone's laptop.
dist

# Version control and editor state
.git
.gitignore
.vscode
.idea
*.swp
.DS_Store

# Local environment files. None are copied by the Dockerfile, but excluding
# them means a real .env can never reach the daemon or an image layer.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Logs and local runtime artefacts
*.log
logs
coverage
.nyc_output
tmp

# CI and docs, none of which affect the runtime image
.github
*.md
